WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Diner's Menu Offer Sparks Debate: Is it Really Sexist?

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:18.000
A restaurant's special ‘girlfriend’ offer is lighting up the internet and sparking debate about possible sexism.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:33.000
It all began in June when Nick Chisler posted a photo on his Facebook page of a menu option titled "My Girlfriend Is Not Hungry."

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:46.000
He took the photo at an Arkansas diner he was eating at, and it gave him a chuckle.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:01:03.000
The cheeky menu option at Mama D's Diner charges $4.25 for adding extra french fries to your order, and a choice of two fried chicken wings or three fried cheese sticks.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Chisler's post quickly became a hot topic on social and mainstream media, getting some 22,000 likes and receiving roughly 5,700 comments.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:38.000
The online comments ran the gamut from a Facebook user who called it "an option you should always order because, you know, girlfriends always get snacky,"

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:52.000
to a Twitter user who said the menu option was a "marriage-saving" kind of idea.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:05.000
But others were not amused. One person posted, "It is usually my boyfriend who isn't hungry," and another said, "It works both ways."

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:27.000
On Eater, a food-review website, Jaya Saxena blasted the offer for fueling the notion that women are "always being coy" and, in a worst-case scenario, are "always secretly looking for attention." The story circulated all the way to the Middle East, where women in Egypt and Jordan gave it a pass. "I'm not offended by it, but since we live in a world where everything and everyone is overly sensitive about the silliest things—I think it should be called the ‘partner option’ rather than the ‘girlfriend option.’ It can work both ways after all," said a 23-year-old Jordanian national.
